From 70974d511a3f66e45cb7639e7deb36790168b912 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kirn Gill II <segin2005@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 9 Jun 2026 23:41:27 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 25/98] ld.so: publish environ before running shared-library
 constructors
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

crt0 assigns environ at program entry — AFTER ld.so's DT_INIT/init_array
pass.  A constructor calling getenv() therefore walked a NULL environ.
Normally latent (libc getenv tolerates it), it became a hard fault with
dtksh: ksh93 interposes getenv(), the canonical-PLT rule binds every
DSO's getenv to the exe's sh_getenv, and a CDE library constructor
calling it crashed in oldgetenv before main (TRAP backtrace: ld.so init
chain -> lib ctor -> getenv -> sh_getenv -> oldgetenv, addr 0x0).

glibc's rtld sets __environ before constructors; do the same: resolve
the canonical `environ` and store envp (from the kernel-built stack)
right before ld_run_init_arrays().

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 sbin/ld.so/ld_main.c | 16 ++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+)

diff --git a/sbin/ld.so/ld_main.c b/sbin/ld.so/ld_main.c
index 11bde090..2d2181f7 100644
--- a/sbin/ld.so/ld_main.c
+++ b/sbin/ld.so/ld_main.c
@@ -566,6 +566,22 @@ ld_u32 ld_main(ld_u32 *initial_stack) {
         for (;;) {}
     }
 
+    /* glibc-rtld parity: publish `environ` BEFORE any constructor runs.
+     * crt0 only assigns it at program entry, which happens after the
+     * DT_INIT/init_array pass below — so a shared-library constructor
+     * calling getenv() (or an interposing getenv, e.g. ksh93/dtksh's
+     * sh_getenv) would otherwise walk a NULL environ and fault.
+     * ld_resolve returns the canonical symbol (the program's copy when
+     * it defines/copies one), which is the same object crt0 assigns. */
+    {
+        ld_u32 envaddr = ld_resolve("environ");
+        if (envaddr) {
+            ld_u32 argc = initial_stack[0];
+            char **envp = (char **)(initial_stack + 1 + argc + 1);
+            *(char ***)envaddr = envp;
+        }
+    }
+
     /* Run constructors in dependency order: deepest deps first
      * (= reverse of load order, since the program is at the head
      * and its deepest dependency is at the tail). */
